  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was a tale of two stores shaped by capacity constraints, not demand weakness.\n\nPort Washington ran on a single groomer and produced $370 per groomer\u201422% below the trailing-30-day baseline of $463. This is a capacity miss, pure and simple. The grooming revenue drop of 4% is modest; the real signal is transactions down 44% and retail revenue down 34%. One active groomer can't service the queue, so customers either leave or buy retail while waiting. The bright spot: add-on attach rate hit 88% versus a typical 60%\u2014your team is disciplined on upsells when they have time. Month-to-date Port Washington is tracking +1.9% against DOW-adjusted expectation, so August performance is real; the calendar didn't carry you. Hicksville yesterday was pure retail noise (no grooming ops, $185 in retail), but MTD it's outpacing expectation by 18.1%\u2014that's solid execution on a smaller capacity footprint. Year-to-date, Port Washington is up 43% and Hicksville is tracking a new store trajectory. Both stores are hitting their productivity ceiling at current staffing.\n\nThe pattern across both locations: you're capacity-constrained, not demand-constrained. Port Washington's lapse pool represents $296k in annual dormant revenue; Hicksville's represents $91k. Reactivation economics favor moving groomer headcount up before pushing customer acquisition harder.\n\n**Capacity & coverage:** Port Washington has 5 groomers scheduled today (15h bathers) and 5 tomorrow (0h bathers); Hicksville has 1 today and 3 tomorrow; next 7 days are 35 groomer-days across both locations (83h bathers). Stock three critical treats across both stores before week-end."
